A useful successor to prompt engineering would therefore study **how intent is distributed across an agentic system**. Call it distributed prompting, environment programming, or something plainer that has not arrived yet. The name can wait. The architecture is already here.

The most interesting test is brutally practical. Take the same capable model and the same three-sentence human request. Put it first in an empty directory. Then put it in a repository with source, tests, `AGENTS.md`, skills, tools, runtime telemetry, reversible sandboxes, and a benchmark. If the second system produces radically stronger work, the difference did not live in the English sentence.

That difference is where programming is moving.

# Limitations and unresolved territory

The evidence here is unusually rich in working artifacts but narrow in provenance. DHH is describing his own practice, his own products, and his own rapidly changing frontier. Several claims in the podcast - especially model comparisons and reported internal outcomes - remain experiential unless the underlying traces are published. The Basecamp architectural regression is important precisely because the public conversation does not expose the full PR sequence needed to audit it.

The argument also draws heavily from software because software is cheap to execute, copy, test, and revert. Agentic practice will look different where actions touch money, health, physical infrastructure, legal status, or irreversible external systems. The ability to "bumble" productively depends on bounded consequences and reliable recovery.

Finally, distributed specification can hide as easily as it can help. When behavior emerges from prompts, skills, source, histories, tool defaults, model priors, and runtime state, causal inspection becomes harder. A short prompt can look simple while carrying an enormous invisible inheritance. Any serious agentic engineering discipline will need provenance tools that show which instruction, artifact, event, or environmental assumption shaped an action.

# Conclusion

A year ago the argument was whether AI could write code worth keeping. DHH's current practice has already moved to a different question: how should a person arrange a world in which code can be generated, tested, revised, diagnosed, and discarded faster than the person can think through every implementation?

The answer emerging from Omarchy and the Fridman conversation is neither a giant prompt nor a new priesthood of prompt engineers. It is a distributed system for interpretation. Put stable knowledge in the environment. Expose tools cleanly. Preserve architectural invariants. Ask for outcomes without dictating every route. Generate artifacts before desire has fully crystallized. Compare alternatives. Make experiments reversible. Route only consequential uncertainty back to the human.

Once those pieces are present, the human can type less because the computer has more to stand on.

And once that happens, the prompt begins to disappear.

